AWPTCM-T5442 (1.0) — DoS: TEARDROP
tb470 IE520 stack (DUT) · port1.0.1 · 2026-09-03 · clean flat-vlan1 bench

RESULT: PASS. Armed port detects the teardrop attack and shuts down; disabled port does not.

Bench adaptation (no IXIA on this bench):
  Original test uses [IXIA]==(port1.0.1)[DUT](port1.0.2)==[IXIA]. Substituted the IXIA with
  tb470 running the lab tool raw-data/test_scripts/tools/denial_of_service/teardrop.py
  (scapy 2.6.1, root). Attack source = tb470 eth3, cabled directly to IE520 port1.0.1
  (LLDP/host-confirmed, see bench-state.md 2026-09-03). One attack path only, so the test's
  "second port has no defense" step is covered by the disable-and-resend negative control
  on the same port (below).

Attack tool invocation (from tb470):
  sudo timeout 8 python3 teardrop.py -s 10.38.215.90 -d <victim-ip> -m <victim-mac> -i eth3
  teardrop.py sends an overlapping-fragment train (IP proto 17, flags=MF, frag offsets
  0,3,23,43,... then a final non-MF frag), looping until killed.

================================================================================
KEY FINDING — the attack must TRANSIT the switch, not be addressed TO it
================================================================================
First attempt addressed the frames to the IE520 itself (-d 10.38.215.66 -m 00:00:cd:37:0d:6f):
  -> Attacks detected: 0, port stayed up. Frames addressed to the switch's own MAC are
     punted to the CPU and bypass the ingress DoS-detection datapath.
Re-aimed at a real host BEHIND the switch -- the x230 (-d 10.38.215.71 -m 00:1a:eb:91:cc:a1),
which transits port1.0.1 -> port2.0.9:
  -> detected immediately. This matches the IXIA model (traffic transiting the DUT).
So: DoS teardrop detection is on transit/datapath ingress, NOT on to-the-CPU traffic.

================================================================================
STEP 1 / arm — dos teardrop action shutdown on port1.0.1
================================================================================
config:  interface port1.0.1 ; dos teardrop action shutdown
--- show dos interface port1.0.1 (armed, pre-attack) ---
DoS settings for interface port1.0.1
-----------------------------------------
Port status         : Enabled
ipoptions           : Disabled
land                : Disabled
ping-of-death       : Disabled
smurf               : Disabled
synflood            : Disabled
teardrop            : Enabled
   Action           : Shutdown port
   Attacks detected : 0

================================================================================
STEP 2 / attack detected + port shut down  (Expected: "% TearDrop Attack detected
and shutdown port1.0.1")
================================================================================
Fired teardrop.py (transit to the x230) for ~8s. Then:
--- show dos interface port1.0.1 ---
   teardrop            : Enabled
   Action           : Shutdown port
   Attacks detected : 2585
   Port status         : Disabled          <- port shut by the action
--- show interface port1.0.1 status ---
port1.0.1   -   err-disable   1   auto auto   10GBASE-T
VERIFY: attack detected (2585) and port1.0.1 shut down (err-disable). PASS.
NOTE: the console banner "% TearDrop Attack detected and shutdown port1.0.1" is emitted
asynchronously to the console and is not retained in the buffered `show log`; the persistent
evidence is the detection counter + the err-disable state above.

================================================================================
STEP 3 / recovery — port comes back on `no shutdown`
================================================================================
config:  interface port1.0.1 ; no shutdown
--- show interface port1.0.1 status ---
port1.0.1   -   connected   1   a-full a-1000   10GBASE-T
VERIFY: err-disable clears on manual no shutdown; link restored.

================================================================================
STEP 4/5 / negative control — defense disabled, same attack, NO shutdown
================================================================================
config:  interface port1.0.1 ; no dos teardrop      (NB: `no dos teardrop`, not
         `no dos teardrop action shutdown` -- the latter is accepted but leaves detection
         ENABLED; observed here as the port shutting down again with the wrong negation.)
--- show dos interface port1.0.1 (after `no dos teardrop`) ---
DoS settings for interface port1.0.1
-----------------------------------------
(empty -- teardrop no longer listed as Enabled)
Re-fired the identical teardrop attack (transit to x230) for ~8s:
--- show interface port1.0.1 status ---
port1.0.1   -   connected   1   a-full a-1000   10GBASE-T
VERIFY: with the defense removed, the same attack causes NO shutdown -- the port stays
connected. Confirms the shutdown in Step 2 was the DoS defense acting, not a side effect. PASS.

================================================================================
STATE AFTER TEST
================================================================================
port1.0.1: up (connected), dos teardrop config REMOVED (back to baseline). All config was
running-config only (reverts on reload). The IE520 still carries the DHCP-snooping test
overlay from T5897 (separate; awaiting a decision to strip or keep).
